<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch9-section-isobaric-coordinates-subsection-thermodynamic-energy-equation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Thermodynamic energy equation</title>

    <p>
        Consider the alternative form of the thermodynamic energy equation,
        <xref ref="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-alt" text="type-global" />
        from <xref ref="ch7-section-thermodynamic-energy-equation" text="custom">Chapter 7</xref>:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-alt-reproduced" number="yes">
            \dot{Q} = c_p \frac{DT}{Dt} - \alpha \frac{Dp}{Dt}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Expanding <m>\dfrac{DT}{Dt}</m> using the
        <xref ref="ch9-section-isobaric-coordinates-subsection-total-derivative" text="custom"> definition of the total derivative in isobaric coordinates</xref>
        and substituting <m>\omega = \dfrac{Dp}{Dt}</m> produce
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-alt-isobaric-start" number="yes">
            \dot{Q}
            = c_p \left(
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial t}
                + u \frac{\partial T}{\partial x}
                + v \frac{\partial T}{\partial y}
                + \omega \frac{\partial T}{\partial p}
            \right)
            - \alpha \omega
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Dividing <xref ref="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-alt-isobaric-start" text="type-global" />
        by <m>c_p</m> and grouping terms with <m>\omega</m> produce
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-alt-isobaric-intermediate" number="yes">
            \frac{\dot{Q}}{c_p}
            = \left(
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial t}
                + u \frac{\partial T}{\partial x}
                + v \frac{\partial T}{\partial y}
            \right)
            - \left(
                \frac{\alpha}{c_p} - \frac{\partial T}{\partial p}
            \right) \omega
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Defining the <term>static stability parameter in isobaric coordinates</term> <m>\sigma_p</m> as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-static-stability-parameter-isobaric" number="yes">
            \sigma_p
            =
            \left(
                \frac{\alpha}{c_p} - \frac{\partial T}{\partial p}
            \right)
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        and substituing this definition into <xref ref="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-alt-isobaric-intermediate" text="type-global" />
        produce
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-alt-isobaric" number="yes">
            \frac{\dot{Q}}{c_p}
            =
            \frac{\partial T}{\partial t}
            + u \frac{\partial T}{\partial x}
            + v \frac{\partial T}{\partial y}
            - \sigma_p \, \omega
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        or
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-thermodynamic-energy-equation-alt-isobaric-subscript" number="yes">
            \frac{\dot{Q}}{c_p}
            = \left(
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial t}
                + u \frac{\partial T}{\partial x}
                + v \frac{\partial T}{\partial y}
            \right)_p
    - \sigma_p \, \omega
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        where the subscript "p" emphasizes that the derivatives are taken on an isobaric surface.
    </p>

    <p>
        It may not be immediately apparent that <m>\sigma_p</m> is a static stability parameter.
        But consider the dry adiabatic lapse rate
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            \Gamma_d = \frac{g}{c_p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        and the environmental lapse rate
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            \Gamma
            =
            -\frac{\partial T}{\partial z}
            =
            -\frac{\partial T}{\partial p} \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
            =
            \rho g \frac{\partial T}{\partial p}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        when assuming hydrostatic balance.
        Then <m>\sigma_p</m> can be rewritten
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \sigma_p
                \amp =
                \left(
                    \frac{\alpha}{c_p} - \frac{\partial T}{\partial p}
                \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp =
                \left(
                    \frac{\alpha}{c_p} - \frac{\Gamma}{\rho g}
                \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp =
                \frac{1}{\rho g} \left(
                    \frac{g}{c_p} - \Gamma
                \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp =
                \frac{1}{\rho g} \left(
                    \Gamma_d - \Gamma
                \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-static-stability-parameter-isobaric-alt" number="yes">
                \amp =
                \frac{\Gamma_d - \Gamma}{\rho g}
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Recall from <xref ref="ch7-section-thermodynamics-review-subsection-stability" text="custom">Chapter 7</xref>
        that <m>\Gamma_d \gt \Gamma</m> indicates local static stability,
        <m>\Gamma_d \lt \Gamma</m> indicates local static instability,
        and <m>\Gamma_d = \Gamma</m> indicates local static neutrality.
        Then <xref ref="eqn-static-stability-parameter-isobaric-alt" text="type-global" />
        makes it apparent that <m>\sigma_p \gt 0</m> indicates local static stability,
        <m>\sigma_p \lt 0</m> indicates local static instability,
        and <m>\sigma_p = 0</m> indicates local static neutrality.
    </p>

    <p>
        Furthermore, the local stability equation given in <xref ref="eqn-stability" text="type-global" />
        of <xref ref="ch7-section-thermodynamics-review-subsection-stability" text="custom">Chapter 7</xref>,
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-stability-reproduced" number="yes">
            \frac{T}{\theta} \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial z}
            =
            \Gamma_d - \Gamma
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        can be expanded and rewritten in isobaric coordinates by taking advantage of the assumption of hydrostatic balance:
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \frac{T}{\theta} \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial z} \amp = \Gamma_d - \Gamma
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \frac{T}{\theta} \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial p} \frac{\partial p}{\partial z} \amp = \Gamma_d - \Gamma
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \frac{T}{\theta} \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial p} (-\rho g) \amp= \Gamma_d - \Gamma
            </mrow>
            <mrow number="yes">
                -\frac{T}{\theta} \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial p} \amp = \frac{\Gamma_d - \Gamma}{\rho g}
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

</subsection>